1. Configuration config = new Configuration();

You said:

Configuration is the class that will help us interact with Hibernate.

Almost.

More accurately:

Configuration is a Hibernate object that we use to prepare Hibernate's configuration.

Think:

Configuration
      ↓
Collect Hibernate settings
      +
Tell Hibernate about entities
      ↓
Build Hibernate environment

It doesn't itself "interact with the database."

The actual database interaction comes later through things like Session.

So:

Configuration
     ↓
Prepares Hibernate
     ↓
SessionFactory
     ↓
Session
     ↓
Database operations
2. config.addAnnotatedClass(Student.class);

Your understanding here is good.

You said:

We allowed Hibernate to know my class.

Exactly. 👍

You're basically telling Hibernate:

"Here is one of the entity classes that you need to manage."

So:

Student.class
     ↓
addAnnotatedClass()
     ↓
Configuration
     ↓
Hibernate knows:
"Student is an entity"

And because Student contains:

@Entity
@Id

Hibernate can understand its mapping.

One small correction:

Don't think:

"this is the class that I suppose to use with the given table"

Think:

"This is an entity class that Hibernate should manage and map to database data."

Because later you can have:

Student.java   → @Entity
Teacher.java   → @Entity
Course.java    → @Entity

and register all of them.

3. config.configure();

You said:

We loaded our configuration file, telling Hibernate the credentials and things needed to understand what happens with the database.

Correct. 👍

More precisely:

config.configure()
       ↓
hibernate.cfg.xml
       ↓
Hibernate reads:
    • database URL
    • username
    • password
    • JDBC driver
    • hbm2ddl settings
    • other configuration

So now Hibernate has two important pieces of information:

             Configuration
                  │
        ┌─────────┴─────────┐
        ↓                   ↓
 Student.class        hibernate.cfg.xml
        │                   │
 "WHAT is my             "HOW do I
  entity?"                configure/connect?"

That's a very good mental model.

4. SessionFactory factory = config.buildSessionFactory();

You skipped this part slightly, and it's important.

After giving Hibernate all the information:

Student.class
      +
hibernate.cfg.xml
      ↓
Configuration

we say:

config.buildSessionFactory();

Meaning roughly:

"Hibernate, take all this configuration and build the thing that will create sessions for me."

That thing is:

SessionFactory

Think of it as:

Configuration
      ↓
buildSessionFactory()
      ↓
SessionFactory
      ↓
can create Sessions
5. Session session = factory.openSession();

Your "opening gate" analogy is actually pretty good. 😄

Just make it slightly more precise:

A Session is Hibernate's working context through which we perform database operations.

So:

SessionFactory
      │
      │ openSession()
      ↓
   Session
      │
      ↓
Database operations

For example:

session.persist(student);

uses that Session.

So your "gate" analogy is okay:

Session
   ↓
Opening a working interaction with Hibernate
   ↓
Perform database operations
6. Transaction transaction = session.beginTransaction();

You said:

Then we started the transaction.

Correct. 👍

Session
   │
   │ beginTransaction()
   ↓
Transaction started

We're saying:

"I'm beginning a group of database operations that should be treated as one transaction."

For your current example, the operation is basically:

save Student
7. session.persist(student);

This is where the actual save request happens.

session.persist(student);

means:

"Hibernate, please make this Student object persistent/save it to the database."

So:

Java object
    │
    │ persist()
    ↓
 Hibernate
    ↓
 JDBC
    ↓
PostgreSQL

Hibernate can then generate the appropriate SQL.

Conceptually:

INSERT INTO student
(id, name, age, city)
VALUES
(1, 'Shubham', 23, 'Pune');
8. transaction.commit();

You said:

Close all the transaction.

Small correction here.

commit() doesn't exactly mean:

"close the transaction."

It means:

"Commit/confirm the changes made during this transaction."

Think:

beginTransaction()
       ↓
Do database operation
       ↓
persist(student)
       ↓
commit()
       ↓
Confirm changes

After commit, that transaction is completed.

9. But you said "then we close the SessionFactory"

This is one thing you should correct in your understanding.

Your current code doesn't actually close the Session or SessionFactory.

You currently have:

transaction.commit();

and then the program ends.

But good Hibernate code should eventually clean up:

session.close();
factory.close();

So the complete lifecycle would conceptually be:

Configuration
      ↓
SessionFactory
      ↓
Session
      ↓
Transaction
      ↓
persist()
      ↓
commit()
      ↓
close Session
      ↓
close SessionFactory

Your current code stops after:

transaction.commit();

So later we'll add the cleanup.

10. Your understanding, rewritten correctly

You can remember your Main.java flow like this:

1. Configuration
       ↓
   Prepare Hibernate configuration




2. addAnnotatedClass(Student.class)
       ↓
   Tell Hibernate about my Student entity




3. configure()
       ↓
   Load hibernate.cfg.xml
       ↓
   Database + Hibernate settings




4. buildSessionFactory()
       ↓
   Build SessionFactory




5. openSession()
       ↓
   Get a Hibernate Session
       ↓
   Working context for database operations




6. beginTransaction()
       ↓
   Start a database transaction




7. persist(student)
       ↓
   Tell Hibernate to save Student




8. commit()
       ↓
   Confirm the transaction




9. close()
       ↓
   Clean up resources

And the overall picture:

              Main.java
                  │
                  ↓
           Configuration
             ↙         ↘
            ↓           ↓
   Student.class    hibernate.cfg.xml
            │           │
            └─────┬─────┘
                  ↓
           SessionFactory
                  │
             openSession()
                  ↓
               Session
                  │
          beginTransaction()
                  ↓
             Transaction
                  │
            persist(student)
                  ↓
              Hibernate
                  ↓
                JDBC
                  ↓
             PostgreSQL
                  ↓
            student table
One sentence to lock into your memory:

Main.java is basically orchestrating the Hibernate lifecycle: configure Hibernate → tell it about entities → build a SessionFactory → open a Session → start a Transaction → perform the database operation → commit → clean up.
